2. Firestore Implementation for Each Table
1. users Collection
Each user has a document inside the users collection.
The user_id is the document ID (Firestore auto-generates unique IDs).
Example Firestore Document:
json
Copy
Edit
{
  "first_name": "John",
  "last_name": "Doe",
  "email": "john@example.com",
  "password_hash": "hashed_password",
  "date_of_birth": "1990-01-01",
  "created_at": "2024-04-22T10:00:00Z"
}
2. classes Subcollection
Instead of a separate classes table, we store classes as a subcollection under each user.
Example Firestore Document in /users/{user_id}/classes/{class_id}
json
Copy
Edit
{
  "class_name": "Math 101",
  "department": "Mathematics",
  "location": "Room 202",
  "start_time": "08:00",
  "end_time": "09:30",
  "days": "Mon,Wed,Fri"
}
3. routes Subcollection
Each user has their own routes subcollection.
Example Firestore Document in /users/{user_id}/routes/{route_id}
json
Copy
Edit
{
  "start_location": "Library",
  "end_location": "Gym",
  "estimated_time": 15,
  "weather_condition": "Sunny",
  "route_details": "Take Main St.",
  "created_at": "2024-04-22T10:05:00Z"
}
