3. Firestore Queries
1. Get a User’s Classes
javascript
Copy
Edit
const userId = "123";  // Firestore document ID
const classesRef = db.collection("users").doc(userId).collection("classes");

classesRef.get().then(snapshot => {
  snapshot.forEach(doc => console.log(doc.data()));
});
2. Add a New Route
javascript
Copy
Edit
const routeRef = db.collection("users").doc(userId).collection("routes").doc();
routeRef.set({
  start_location: "Library",
  end_location: "Gym",
  estimated_time: 15,
  weather_condition: "Sunny",
  route_details: "Take Main St.",
  created_at: new Date()
});
4. Why This Firestore Design?
✅ Scalable: Users can have unlimited classes, routes, logs, etc.
✅ Faster Queries: Data is grouped by user, so fetching all user-related info is efficient.
✅ NoSQL-Friendly: Firestore avoids complex joins by using subcollections.

Final Thoughts
SQL → NoSQL conversion requires thinking in terms of documents & subcollections.
Firebase Firestore is schema-less, but using structured subcollections helps mimic SQL relations.
You can still enforce data validation rules using Firestore security rules.
